  "core_message": "All 155 aboard survived because the pilots chose to glide into the Hudson.",
  "mood": "curious",
  "title": "2,800‑ft Engine Failure: 155 Lives Saved in Four Minutes",
  "description": "On 15 January 2009, US Airways Flight 1549 lost both engines after a goose strike and glided into the Hudson River. In less than four minutes the pilots turned the aircraft into a life‑saving water landing. \n\nKey facts: the flight was 15 minutes into its climb when the engines failed, the aircraft hit the river at 125 knots, and every passenger and crew member survived.\n\nWhy it mattered: it proved that a skilled pilot can turn a catastrophic failure into a miraculous rescue.\n\nBased on the Wikipedia article \"US Airways Flight 1549\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Airways_Flight_1549), licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.\n\nImages from Wikimedia Commons:\nUS Airways Flight 1549 in Hudson cropped.JPG (Izno , cropped by Falcorian, CC BY-SA 3.0): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Airways_Flight_1549_in_Hudson_cropped.JPG\nUS Air Flight 1549 rescue efforts.jpg (Photo by Chris Gardner, USACE New York District Public Affairs, Public domain): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Air_Flight_1549_rescue_efforts.jpg\nUSAirways-1549 lifting out of Hudson.jpg (Spyropk, CC BY-SA 3.0): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USAirways-1549_lifting_out_of_Hudson.jpg",
